  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}-8}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(3,5)$ is $4.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-10$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $4$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=4.$",
